Who is the most important person in the kingdom?
At that time the disciples came to Jesus. And they asked Him, "Who is the most important in the kingdom of heaven?"
Jesus called a little child and had him stand among them.
Jesus said, "What I have to tell you is true. You must change and become like little children. If you don't, you will not enter the kingdom of heaven."
Everyone who becomes humble like this child is the greatest in the kingdom of heaven.
"Whoever welcomes such a little child in my name, welcomes me."
But what if one of these little ones who believe in me causes them to sin? If he causes them to sin, it would be better for him to have a large millstone hung around his neck and to be drowned in the depth of the sea.
How terrible on the world will be those things that lead people into sin! Such things must come. But how terrible for those who cause them!
Your hands or your feet are causing you to slip into sins, so cut them off and throw them away from you. It is better for you to be admitted to Paradise because of either one of them than to be thrown into Hellfire because of both of them. And you will not have wronged yourselves then. And Allah is Forgiving and Merciful. The fire ignites Hell once again.
If anyone wants to gouge out his eye in order to see, let him gouge it out and throw it away. It is better for him to enter the kingdom of God with one eye, than to be thrown into hell with two eyes. The story of the lost sheep.
Look, don't look at any of these little ones with contempt, look, I tell you, they have angels in heaven who rise at any time to see my Father in heaven.
<.>
What is your opinion? Imagine a man who owns 100 sheep, and one of them goes astray. Should he not leave the ninety-nine sheep on the mountain? Should he not go and search for the one that has gone astray?
What I am going to tell you is true. If he finds that lost sheep, he will be happier with it than he is with those 99 that have not strayed.
And it is so with your father in heaven. He does not want any of these little ones to perish. When someone wrongs you
If your brother wrongs you, go to him. Tell him what he did wrong. Make that between the two of you. If he listens to you, he has returned to you.
But what if he will not listen to you? Take one or two more persons with you. The Book says: "Every fact must be confirmed by the words of two or three witnesses" (Deut. 19:15).
But what if he also refuses to listen to witnesses? Then tell the church. And what if he refuses to listen even to the church? Then do not treat him as a brother. Treat him as you would treat someone who does not believe or a tax collector.
What I will tell you is true. What you bind on earth will be bound in heaven. What you loose on earth will be loosed in heaven.
And again, here is what I tell you. If two of you agree on earth about something they ask me, I will do it for them from myself.
"Wherever two or three are gathered in my name, I am there among them." The servant who did not show mercy.
Peter came to Jesus and asked, "Lord, how many times must I forgive my brother when he sins against me? Up to seven times?"
Jesus said, "I tell you, not seven times, but seventy-seven times.
The kingdom of heaven is like a king who wanted to settle accounts with his servants.
And the king began to do that, and a man was presented to him who owed him millions of dollars.
The man could not pay. So his master gave the order. The man, his wife, his children, and everything he possessed, had to be sold to pay his debt.
The servant knelt before him. "Give me time," he pleaded. "I will pay everything back."
His lord felt pity for him. He forgave him the debt he owed and set him free.
But the slave went out and found one of the other slaves, to whom he owed a few dollars. So he seized him and began to strangle him. 'Pay me what you owe me!' he said.
The other server fell to his knees. 'Give me time,' he pleaded with him. 'I will return the favor.'
But the first servant refused. Instead, he went and had the man thrown into prison. And there he would stay until he could pay back what he owed.
The other servants saw what happened. This disturbed them greatly. They went and told their master everything that had happened.
Then the Lord called His first servant and said, "You wicked servant! I forgave you your debt that you could not pay."
Haven't you been merciful to the other servant just as you have been merciful to me?
His master, in a fit of rage, threw him to the jailers. He would be punished until he repaid all his debts.
Thus your Father who is in heaven treats everyone, unless you forgive your brothers from your hearts.
Jesus teaches about divorce.
When Jesus had finished saying these things, he left Galilee and went into the region of Judea beyond the Jordan River.
Large crowds followed him. He healed them there.
Some Pharisees came to test him. They asked, "Is it lawful for a man to divorce his wife for just any reason?"
Jesus said, "Have they not read in the Scriptures: 'God created man in His own image; in the image of God He created him; male and female He created them' (Genesis 1:27)"
He said, "Therefore a man shall leave his father and mother and be joined to his wife, and they shall become one flesh" (Genesis 2:24).
What God has joined together, let no one separate.
They asked, "So why did Moses command the man to write a divorce certificate for his wife and send her away?"
Jesus answered, "Moses allowed you to divorce because of the hardness of your hearts. But it was not like this in the beginning."
This is what I tell you. Anyone who divorces his wife and marries another commits adultery. A man should not divorce his wife unless she is not faithful to him.
The disciples said to him, "If such is the case between husband and wife, it is better not to marry."
Jesus answered, "Not everyone can accept the idea of remaining single. Only those whom someone has helped to live without marrying can accept it.
Some men cannot have children because they were born that way. Some had others make them that way. And others made themselves that way to serve the Kingdom of Heaven. And whoever can accept living that way, must do so." The little children come to Jesus.
Some people brought two children to Jesus. They wanted him to lay his hands on the children and pray for them. But the disciples told the people to stop.
Jesus said, "Let the little children come to me, and do not hinder them, for the kingdom of God belongs to such as these."
Jesus laid his hands on them. Then he left there. Jesus and the Rich Young Man
A man came to Jesus. And he asked, "Teacher, what good thing must I do to get eternal life?"
"Why do you ask me about what is good?" Jesus answered. "There is only one who is good. If you want to enter into the kingdom, obey the commandments."
"Which?" the man asked. Jesus said, "Do not murder. Do not commit adultery. Do not steal. Do not give false testimony against your neighbor."
"Honor your father and your mother." (Exodus 20:12-16; Deuteronomy 5:16-20) and "Love your neighbor as yourself." (Leviticus 19:18)
"I have obeyed all those commands," said the young man. "What should I do next?"
Jesus answered, "If you want to be perfect, go, sell your possessions and give the money to the poor, and you will have treasure in heaven. Then come, follow me."
When the young man heard that, he left sadly. He was very rich.
Then Jesus said to his disciples: "What I tell you now is true. It is hard for those who have wealth to enter the Kingdom of Heaven."
And I tell you again, it is easier for a camel to go through the eye of a needle. It is easier for a rich person to enter the kingdom of God.
When the disciples heard this, they confirmed it. So they said, "Who then can be saved?"
Jesus looked at them and said, "What is impossible for man is possible for God."
Peter answered him, "We have left everything to follow you! What will be our reward?"
"I am not yet worthy to even say this to you, that it will be so." said Jesus. "At the time when all things are made new, the Son of the Man will sit on His glorious throne. Then those who follow Me will sit on twelve thrones, judging the twelve tribes of Israel."
Everyone who has left a house or family or field because of me will receive 100 times more. And they will also receive eternal life.
Many who are first will be last, and many who are last will be first.
The Story of the Workers in the Vineyard
The kingdom of heaven is like a man who owned a vineyard. He went out early in the morning to hire workers for his vineyard.
He agreed to pay them the usual wage for a day's work. Then he sent them into his vineyard.
He went out again around nine in the morning. He saw others standing in the city market doing nothing.
He said to them, "Go also and work in my vineyard, and I will give you a just wage."
They went out. "He went out again around noon and at three and did the same thing.
He went out around five o'clock and found others standing around. He asked them, "Why have you been standing here all day doing nothing?"
They answered, "Because no one has asked us to work." He told them, "You also, go into my vineyard."
When evening came, the owner of the vineyard spoke to the supervisor of the workers. He said, "Call the workers and give them their pay. Start with the others who were hired. Then go to the first ones."
The workers who were hired around five o'clock arrived. Each of them received their usual day's wage.
Therefore, when those who had been hired first came, they expected to receive more. But each of them also received the pay for a normal day.
When they received it, they began to complain about the landlord.
'These people who were recently hired only worked for one hour,' they said. 'You paid them the same amount that you paid us. We did most of the work. We were under the hot sun all day.'
One of them the master answered: My friend, I am being honest with you. Did you not agree to work for the usual day's wage?
Take your money and leave. I want to give the person I recently hired the same salary I gave you.
Do I not have the right to do as I please with my money? Do you feel cheated because I have given generously to others?
"Therefore the last will be first, and the first will be last." Jesus, once again, speaks of his impending death.
Jesus was going up to Jerusalem. And on the way, he took the twelve disciples aside to talk to them.
"We are going up to Jerusalem," he said. "The Son of Man will be delivered over to the chief priests and the scribes. They will condemn him to death."
Then they hand him over to the people who are not Jewish, who mock him and flog him, and then they crucify him on a cross. And on the third day, he rises from the dead! A mother begging Jesus
The mother of the sons of Zebedee came to Jesus with her sons. She knelt down and asked him for a request.
"What do you want?" Jesus asked. "Promise me," she said, "that one of my sons will sit at your right in your kingdom. And promise me that the other will sit at your left."
"You do not know what you are asking," Jesus said to them. "Can you drink the cup of suffering that I am to drink?" "Yes, we can," they answered.
Jesus said to them, "Assuredly, you will drink from my cup; and how I wish that none of you would drink it! But I have no choice but to accept the cup the Father has given me. And how I wish that none of you would have to drink from it! For my part, my Father is glorified by this. For you are the ones who have told me of the Lord's salvation, the acts which he performed in Egypt, and in the Red Sea, and in the wilderness for forty years; and that you might possess the land which the Lord swore to our forefathers, to Abraham, to Isaac, and to Jacob. And not only this, but also that from the kingdom of the tribes of Israel, the Lord our God will raise up for the tribes of Israel a prophet, like me, from among their brethren; to whom he will give commandments, just as he gave me commandments, just as he gave me commandments, to whom he will speak face to face, as a man speaks to his friend. And the tribes of Israel shall not be rebellious against the one who rules over them, but shall fear the Lord their God, and shall serve him, and shall hold fast to him, and shall not forsake the covenant which they made before him at the first. For the Lord their God will raise up for them a prophet like me, from among their brethren, to whom he will give commandments, just as he gave me commandments, just as he gave me commandments, to whom he will speak face to face, as a man speaks to his friend. And the tribes of Israel shall not be rebellious against the one who rules over them, but shall fear the Lord their God, and shall serve him, and shall hold fast to him, and shall not forsake the covenant which they made before him at the first. For the Lord their God will raise up for them a prophet like me, from among their brethren, to whom he will give commandments, just as he gave me commandments, just as he gave me commandments, to whom he will speak face to face, as a man speaks to his friend. And the tribes of Israel shall not be rebellious against the one who rules over them, but shall fear the Lord their God, and shall serve him, and shall hold fast to him, and shall not forsake the covenant which they made before him at the first. For the Lord their God will raise up for them a prophet like me, from among their brethren, to whom he will give commandments, just as he gave me commandments, just as he gave me commandments, to whom he will speak face to face, as a man speaks to his friend. And the tribes of Israel shall not be rebellious against the one who rules over them, but shall fear the Lord their God, and shall serve him, and shall hold fast to him, and shall not forsake the covenant which they made before him at the first. For the Lord their God will raise up for them a prophet like me, from among their brethren, to whom he will give commandments, just as he gave me commandments, just as he gave me commandments, to whom he will speak face to face, as a man speaks to his friend. And the tribes of Israel shall not be rebellious against the one who rules over them, but shall fear the Lord their God, and shall serve him, and shall hold fast to him, and shall not forsake the covenant which they made before him at the first. For the Lord their God will raise up for them a prophet like me, from among their brethren, to whom he will give commandments, just as he gave me commandments, just as he gave me commandments, to whom he will speak face to face, as a man speaks to his friend. And the tribes of Israel shall not be rebellious against the one who rules over them, but shall fear the Lord their God, and shall serve him, and shall hold fast to him, and shall not forsake the covenant which they made before him at the first. For the Lord their God will raise up for them a prophet like me, from among their brethren, to whom he will give commandments, just as he gave me commandments, just
The other ten pupils heard that. They were angry with the two brothers.
Jesus called his disciples and said, "You know that the rulers of the Gentiles lord it over them, and their great ones exercise authority over them.
Do not be like this. Rather, whoever among you wants to be important must be your servant.
And whoever wants to be first, let him be your slave.
Be like the Son of Man. He did not come to be served, but to serve others. He came to give his life as a ransom for many." Two blind men receive their sight.
Jesus and his disciples were leaving Jericho. A large crowd followed them.
There were two blind men sitting by the road. When they heard that Jesus was passing by, they cried out, saying, "Lord, son of David, have mercy on us!"
The crowd ordered them to stop. They told them to be silent. But the two men shouted even louder, "Lord! Son of David! Have mercy on us!"
Jesus stopped and called to them, "What do you want me to do for you?" he asked.
"Our Lord," they answered, "we want to see."
Jesus felt deep compassion for them. He touched their eyes, and they saw immediately. They followed him.
Jesus enters Jerusalem.
When they all approached Jerusalem, they came to Bethphage, which was on the Mount of Olives. Jesus sent two disciples ahead.
He said to them, "Go to the village before you. As soon as you enter it, you will find a donkey tied up, and you will find its foal with it. Shave them both and bring me here."
If anyone tells you something, say that the Lord needs them. The owner will send them immediately.
This happened so that what came out of the mouth of the Prophet would be fulfilled. He says,
"Tell this to the daughter of Zion: 'Behold, your king is coming to you, righteous and victorious, lowly and riding on a donkey, on a colt, the foal of a donkey.''" (Zechariah 9:9)
The disciples went and did what Jesus had commanded them.
They brought a donkey and a female foal. They placed their cloaks on them. Then Jesus sat on the two cloaks.
A very large crowd of people spread out on the road and opened their coats. Others cut branches from trees and spread them on the road.
Some people went ahead of him, and some followed. And they all cried out, saying, "Blessed is he who comes in the name of the Lord!" (Psalm 118:26) "Blessed in the highest heavens!"
When Jesus entered Jerusalem, he shook the whole city. And the people asked, "Who is this?"
And the crowd answered, "This is Jesus of Nazareth, the prophet." Jesus Cleanses the Temple
Jesus entered the temple court. And he began to drive out those who were buying and selling there. And he overturned the tables of the money changers, and the chairs of those who sold pigeons.
He said to them: "It is written that the Lord said: 'My house shall be called a house of prayer for all the people'" (Isaiah 56:7). "But you have made it a den of robbers" (Jeremiah 7:11)."
The blind and those who had deformities came to Jesus at the temple. And he healed them there.
The chief priests and the teachers of the law saw the wonderful miracles he did. They also saw the children in the temple area shouting, "Praise God! Bless the son of David!" But the chief priests and teachers of the law were angry when they saw all this.
"Do you hear what these children are saying?" they asked Him. "Yes," Jesus answered. "Have you not read that in the book? It says: 'Because He has set His love firmly on Me, therefore He will deliver Me from the strivings of the people, and I will be delivered from the hater and the envier.' (Psalm 18:19-20)
Then Jesus went out from the crowd and went into the house of Simon the leper. He spent the night there. The disciples of John asked him, "Why do we and the Pharisees fast, but your disciples do not fast?" Jesus said, "The attendants of the bridegroom cannot mourn as long as the bridegroom is with them, but they will later fast. No one sews a patch of unshrunk cloth onto an old garment; otherwise, the new patch will pull away from the old garment, and the tear will become even worse. The disciples of John also used to fast. People came to Jesus and saw the blind man walking with them, and they asked, "What happened to this man?" Some people said, "He is John the Baptist; he has risen from the dead, and that is why these miraculous signs are happening through him." Others said, "He is Elijah." And others said, "He is a prophet, like one of the prophets of old." But when Herod heard this, he was deeply disturbed; and he suspected that John had somehow been raised from the dead. Herod had arrested John and bound him and put him in prison because of Herodias, the wife of Herod's brother Philip. John had told Herod, "It is not lawful for you to have your brother's wife." Herodias harbored a grudge against John and wanted to kill him, but she could not do so. When Herod celebrated his birthday with a great feast for his nobles and military commanders and for the leading men of Galilee, the daughter of Herodias came forward and danced, and she pleased Herod and his guests. The king said to the girl, "Whatever you ask of me, I will give you." He swore to her, "As surely as God lives, what you ask for I will give you." She went out and said to her mother, "What should I ask for?" Her mother replied, "The head of John the Baptist." The girl went back in quickly to the king and stood before him. "I will pay you whatever you ask," the king said to her. "The head of John the Baptist," she answered. The king was extremely distressed, but because of his oaths and his guests, he did not want to refuse her. Immediately the king sent a soldier of the guard with orders to bring John's head. The soldier went out and met John, who was accompanied by two disciples. The soldier came up to John and said, "Rabbi, that prophet who used to speak to you in the temple - remember, he is the one who turned you in." John said to him, "There is no need to be concerned about that; it is God's will that this should happen, and it is for this reason that I came." The soldier, hearing these words, was deeply moved and asked John to pray for him, which he did. Then the soldier, taking John's head, left. When John's two disciples saw what had happened, they were deeply grieved and went to the place where John's body lay. There they remained for a considerable time, mourning and weeping over John's death.
Early in the morning, Jesus was going to Jerusalem. He was hungry.
He saw a fig tree by the road. He went up to it, but he found nothing on it except leaves. Then he said to it, "Never bear fruit again!" And the fig tree withered immediately.
When the disciples saw this, they were perplexed. "How did the olive tree wither so quickly?" they asked.
Jesus said, "I tell you this, it is through the act of giving that you will receive your reward. And when you pray, your prayers will be heard. And when you ask the mountain to cast down water and fire upon itself, it will be done for you. But as for believing, what greater thing is there than what is written, 'It is by believing that things are brought to completion, not by the performance of works'?"
"If you believe, you will receive whatever you ask for in prayer." Doubting Jesus' authority
Jesus entered the temple courts. As he taught, the chief priests and the teachers of the law came up to him. "By what authority are you doing these things?" they asked. "And who gave you this authority?"
Jesus answered and said, "I also will ask you a question. If you answer me, I also will tell you by what authority I do these things.
From where did the baptism of John come? Was it from heaven, or from men?" They disputed with one another, saying, "If we say, 'From heaven,' he will say, 'Why did you not believe him?'
But what if we said, "Among men"? We are afraid of people. Everyone thought that John was a prophet.
They said to him, "We do not know." Jesus said to them, "Nor will I tell you by what authority I do these things." The Parable of the Two Sons.
What do you think about this? There was a man who had two sons. He went to the first one and said, "My son, go and work today in the vineyard."
"I will not," the son answered. But afterwards he changed his mind and went.
Then the father went to the other son. He told him the same thing. The son replied, "I will, Father." But he did not go.
Which of the two sons did the will of his father?" They said, "The first." Jesus said to them, "Truly, I say to you, tax collectors and prostitutes enter the kingdom of God ahead of you."
John came to show you the right way to live. But you did not believe him. The tax collectors and prostitutes, however, did believe him. You saw that. But even then you did not stop your sins and believe him. The story of the tenants.
Listen to another story. A man owned some land, so he planted vineyards. He put a wall around them. And he dug a reservoir in them for pressing wine. He also built a watchtower. And he rented the vineyards to some farmers. Then he traveled on a journey.
When the harvest time approached, he sent his servants to the tenants. And he commanded the servants to gather his share of the fruits.
But the tenants seized his servants. They beat one of them. They killed another. They threw a stone at a third, intending to kill him.
Then the man sent other servants to the tenants. He sent more than he had sent at first. The tenants treated them in the same way.
And finally, He sent His Son to them and said, "They will respect my Son."
But the tenants saw the son coming. They said to each other, "This is the one who will take over all the owner's possessions someday. Let's kill him. Then everything will be ours."
And they took him and cast him out of the vineyard. Then they killed him.
When did the owner of the vineyard come? What is he doing with these tenants?
"They will tear down those evil people," they answered. "Then the field will be rented to others. They will give him his share of the crop at harvest time."
Jesus said to them, "Has it not been written in the Law, 'The stone which the builders rejected has become the head of the corner; This is from the Lord, and it is wonderful in our eyes'? (Psalm 118:22, 23)"
Here is what I say to you. The kingdom of God will be taken from you, and it will be given to a people that produces its fruits.
Everyone who falls on that rock will be shattered into pieces. But the rock will crush anyone it falls on.
The chief priests and the Pharisees heard reports about Jesus. They knew he was speaking about them.
So they looked for a way to arrest him. But they feared the crowd. The people believed that Jesus was a prophet.
The Wedding Dinner Story
Jesus told them more stories. He said:
This is the parable of the Kingdom of Heaven. A king prepared a wedding feast for his son.
Servants were sent to those who had been invited to the dinner. The servants told them to come, but they refused.
Then he sent out more servants than the first. He said, "Tell those who are invited, 'I have prepared my dinner, my oxen and fattened cattle are killed. All is ready for you. Come to the dinner I prepared.'"
But the people did not care. One of them went to his field. And another went to his business.
The rest seized his servants. They treated him badly and then killed him.
The king was furiously angry. He sent his army to destroy them. They killed those murderers and burned their city.
Then the king said to his servants, "The reading for the wedding has been prepared. But those whom I invited were not worthy of attending."
Go to the street corners. Invite to dinner anyone you find.
The servants went out into the streets and gathered everyone they found, good and bad people. Soon the wedding hall was filled with guests.
The king entered to see the guests. He noticed a man there who was not wearing wedding clothes.
"My friend, how did you get in here without your wedding clothes?" The man found nothing to say.
Then he gestured to his servants, and they restrained him and threw him into the cloud. They went out with him to a distant place. And there the people weep and bite their fingers.
Many are called, but few are chosen. Is it right to pay taxes to Caesar?
The Pharisees went out and immediately took counsel with the Herodians against Jesus, to trap him in his words.
They sent their followers to him. They sent the Herodians with them. "Teacher," they said, "we know that you are a good and honest man. You truly seek to learn the ways of God. You do not let others tell you what to do or say. It does not matter how important they are."
So tell us, what do you think? Is it right to pay taxes to Caesar or not?"
But Jesus perceived their wicked thoughts. He said to them, "You hypocrites! What schemes are you trying to use to entrap me?"
"Show me the money that people use to pay taxes." And they brought him a silver coin.
He asked them, "Whose picture is this? And whose words are these?"
"For Caesar," they said. Then he said to them, "Render to Caesar the things that are Caesar's, and render to God the things that are God's."
When they heard this, it was distressing to them. So they departed, and they were negotiating. A marriage when the dead rise.
On that day, the Samaritans came to Jesus with a question. They do not believe that people rise from the dead.
"O teacher," they said, "this is what Moses commanded us. If a man dies without leaving children, his brother shall marry his widow. So that he may give birth to children who will carry his name."
There were seven brothers among us. The first got married and died. Since he had no children, he left his wife for his brother.
The same thing happened to the second brother, and the third brother. It even happened to the seventh brother.
Finally, the woman died.
Well, when the dead rise, who will be his wife? All seven were married to her.
Jesus answered, "You are mistaken, because you do not know the Scriptures or the power of God."
When the dead rise, they will not marry. Nor will their fathers give them away in marriage. They will be like the angels in heaven.
What about the dead being raised? Have you not read what God has revealed to you?
He said, "I am God, the One who appeared in a burning bush to Moses; the One who appeared to Abraham in a vision; the One who appeared to Isaac in a vision; and the One who appeared to Jacob in a vision." (Exodus 3:6) "Now the God of the living, not the God of the dead, for all are alive to Him."
When the crowd heard this, they were bewildered at what he was teaching. Which commandment is the most important?
The Pharisees heard that the Samaritans could not answer Jesus. So the Pharisees gathered together.
One of them was an expert in the law. He tested Jesus with a question.
«Teacher,» he asked, «which commandment in the law is the most important?»
Jesus answered, "Blessed are those who love the Lord and love His mercy" (Deuteronomy 6:5).
This is the first and most important commandment.
And a similar one: "Love your neighbor as yourself" (Leviticus 19:18).
All that was written in the Torah and the Gospel about these two commandments. Who is the son of Christ?
The Pharisees met together. Jesus asked them,
What do you think about the Messiah? Who are his builders?" They said, "The son of David."
He said to them, "Then why does David call him 'Lord'?" The Holy Spirit spoke through David himself. David said,
'The Lord said to my Lord, "Sit at my right hand until I make your enemies your footstool"' (Psalm 110:1)'
If David calls him "sir", how can he be his son?
No one could answer him with a single word. And from that day on, no one dared to ask him any questions.
Jesus condemns the Pharisees and teachers of the law.
Jesus spoke to the crowds and to his disciples.
"The scribes and Pharisees sit in Moses' seat," He said.
Therefore, you must obey them. Do everything they command you to do. But do not do what they do. They do not practice what they preach.
They tie heavy things on the shoulders of others. But they themselves do not wish to lift a single finger to move them.
All they do is done to be seen by others. And on their foreheads and arms they put small boxes containing verses from the Bible. And they make the boxes very wide. And they make the strings hanging from their coats very long.
They love to sit in the place of honor at banquets. They also love to be in the most important seats in temples.
They like to be welcomed into the markets. They like to be called "Rabbi".
But none of you should be called 'Lord.' You have only one Lord, and you are all brothers."
Do not say 'father' to anyone on earth. For you have one Father, and He is in heaven.
None of you should be called teacher. You have one teacher, the Christ.
The most important people to you are your servants.
He who exalts himself will be humbled, and he who humbles himself will be exalted.
Woe to you, teachers of the law and the prophets! You are hypocrites! You lock up the kingdom of heaven in men's faces. You do not enter, nor do you allow those who would enter to do so.
<.>
"How awful it is for you, teachers of the law and you, Pharisees! You are hypocrites! You go all over the world to convert one person to your religion. Then you make him twice as much a child of hell as you are.
"How terrible that was for you blind guides! You say, 'If anyone has sworn by the temple, no oath has been sworn.' But whoever has sworn by the gold in the temple, their oath stands.' "
You are blind and stupid! Which is more important? The gold? Or is it the temple that makes the gold sacred?
You also say, 'Whoever swears by the altar, is under no obligation. But whoever swears by the offering on it, is under obligation.'
Blind guides! Which is more important? The gift? Or the altar to which the gift makes its way, becoming sacred?
For whoever swears by the altar, swears by it and by everything on it.
And everyone who swears by the name of the temple, swears by his name and by the one who dwells in it.
And everyone who swears by heaven swears by the name of God's throne and by the one who sits on it.
"How wretched were you teachers of the law and Pharisees! You impostors! You give God a tenth of your garden herbs, such as mint, dill, and cumin. But you have neglected the more important matters of the law, such as justice, mercy, and faithfulness. You should have practiced the latter without neglecting to do the former."
You blind guides! You filter out the smallest gnat from your drink. But you swallow a whole camel!
Woe to you, teachers of the law and you, Pharisees, hypocrites! You are like tombs, which look beautiful on the outside but inside are full of dead bones and everything unclean. Even so, on the outside you appear righteous to people, but inside you are full of hypocrisy and lawlessness.
O blind Pharisee! First cleanse the inside of the cup and of the platter, and then the outside of the cup and of the platter shall be clean also.
How awful it was for you, teachers of the law and Pharisees! You are hypocrites! You are like whitewashed tombs. They look beautiful on the outside. But inside they are full of the bones of the dead. So also, on the outside they appear to people to be righteous. But inside, on the inside, they are full of hypocrisy and lawlessness.
This is the case with you. From the outside, it looks like you are doing the right thing. But on the inside, you are full of wrong. You pretend to be what you are not.
How awful it is for you, teachers of the law and you Pharisees! You hypocrites! You build tombs for the prophets. You decorate the tombs of the righteous.
And you say: If only we had lived in the times of those who lived before us, we would not have followed their path, nor would we have accepted what they were doing.
You have testified against yourselves, and you are now testifying that you are the sons of those who killed the prophets.
Complete the sins that those before you began!
O nest of vipers! How will you escape the punishment of hell?
Therefore, I send you prophets and wise men and teachers. You will kill some of them, and you will crucify others on a cross. And you will flog some in your churches. And you will drive them out of town to town.
Therefore you will pay for all the blood of the holy ones that has been poured out on the earth, from the blood of righteous Abel to the blood of Zechariah, the son of Barachiah. Zechariah, whom you murdered between the temple and the altar.
What I will tell you is true. All of this will happen to those who are living now.
Jerusalem! Jerusalem! You who kill the prophets and throw stones to kill those sent to you. How often I have wanted to gather your people, as a hen gathers her brood under her wings. But you would not let me!
Look, you left your house empty.
I tell you, you will not see me until you say, "Blessed is he who comes in the name of the Lord." (Psalm 118:26)
Signs of the Hour
Jesus left the temple. As he was walking away, his disciples came to him. They wanted to call his attention to the temple buildings.
Do you see all these things? Jesus asked. What I tell you is true. Not one stone will be left here on another. Every stone will be thrown down.
Jesus was sitting on the Mount of Olives. Then the disciples came to him privately. "Tell us," they said, "when will this happen? And what will be the sign of your coming? And what will be the sign of the end?"
Jesus answered, "Be on your guard! Beware of the yeast of the Pharisees and Sadducees."
Many will come in my name. They will say, "I am the Christ!" They will deceive many people.
You will hear of wars. You will also hear people talking about wars of the future. Do not be alarmed. Such things must happen. But the end is not yet.
There will be war between nations. And there will be war between kingdoms. And the people will suffer from hunger. And earthquakes will occur in many places.
All these are the beginning of the birth pangs.
"ثُمَّ يُسْلَمُونَكَ لِيَضْرِبُوكَ وَيَقْتُلُوكَ ۚ وَيُصَلُّونَ عَلَيْكَ ۙ وَيَشْتَرُونَ الَّذِينَ يَضِلُّونَ مِنْكَ لِيَعْلَمَ اللَّهُ مَا يَقُولُونَ ۗ إِنَّ الْمُؤْمِنِينَ لَيْسَ لَهُمْ أَنْ يَتَرَدَّدُوا فِيكَ ۗ أَمْرٌ قَدْ قَضَىٰ اللَّهُ أَنْ يَقُولُوا مَا أَنْزَلَ اللَّهُ مِنْ شَيْءٍ ۚ إِنْ أَنْتَ إِلَّا نَذِيرٌ".
At that time, many will turn away from their faith. They will hate each other. They will hand each other over to their enemies.
Many false prophets will appear. They will deceive many people.
Because evil is growing, the love of most people will grow cold.
But whoever endures to the end will be saved.
This great news about the kingdom will be preached throughout the world. It will be a witness to all nations. Then the end will come.
The prophet Daniel spoke of "the abomination that makes desolate" (Daniel 9:27; 11:31; 12:11) You will see someday standing in the holy place. Let the reader understand this.
Then those in Galilee will go out to the mountains.
No one on the roof is allowed to go down to their home to get anything.
Nobody in the square should go back to get their coat.
How dreadful those days will be for pregnant women! How dreadful they will be for nursing mothers!
I hope you don't have to flee in winter or on a Saturday.
There will be terrible suffering in those days. It will be worse than anything else that has ever happened in the world since the beginning of time. And there will never be anything like it again.
If time did not shorten, no one would live, but so that the people of God may live, it shortens.
At that time, someone will tell you, "Look! Here is the Messiah!" or "He is over there!" Do not believe it.
False christs and lying priests will appear. They will perform great signs and miracles. They will seek to seduce God's chosen people if possible.
Look, I already told you.
If anyone tells you, 'He is out in the wilds,' do not go out there; or if someone says to you, 'He is in the inner chambers,' do not believe him.
Lightning, seen from the east, will be visible in the west; and so it will be when the Son of Man comes.
Seagulls will gather wherever there is a carcass.
And after the terrible suffering in those days, 'the sun will be darkened, and the moon will not give its light. The stars will fall from the sky, and the heavens will be shaken.' (Isaiah 13:10; 34:4)
"Then the sign of the Son of Man will appear in heaven, and all the nations of the earth will mourn. They will see the Son of Man coming on the clouds of heaven, with power and great glory."
He sends His angels with a loud trumpet call, like a violent storm cloud, and He gathers the chosen people from every direction, four. And He gathers them from one end of the sky to the other.
Learn from the fig tree. As soon as its branches become soft and it puts forth leaves, you know that summer is near.
Likewise, when you see all those things happening, you know the end is near. It's at the door.
What I am about to tell you is right. The people living at that time will certainly not die until all these things happen.
If the heaven and earth were to vanish, God would replace them. Say, "Indeed, my Lord extends provision for whomever He wills of His servants, and He restricts it for him; and whatever you have spent
No one knows that day or hour. Not even the angels in heaven know. The Son does not know. Only the Father knows.
Remember how it was in the days of Noah. It will be the same when the Son of Man comes.
In the days before the flood, people were eating and drinking. They were marrying. They were giving their daughters in marriage. They were doing all those things until the day that Noah entered the ark.
They did not know what was going to happen until the flood came and took them all. That is how it will be when the Son of Man comes.
There will be two men in a field. One will be taken, and the other will be left.
Two women will grind using a hand mill. One will be taken and the other will be left.
Take care of yourself. You do not know where the day will come when your Lord will come to you.
You must understand something. Imagine that the homeowner knows at what time of night the thief comes. Then he would keep guard over his house. He would not leave his house open.
Therefore you also must be ready; for the Son of Man will come at an hour when you do not expect.
Let us suppose that a master has put one of his servants in charge of the other servants in his house. The servant's job is to give them their food at the proper time. The master wants a faithful and wise servant for this.
It will be better for the servant if the master finds the servant working at his task upon the master's return.
What I am going to tell you is true. The captain will put this slave in charge of everything he owns.
But what if this slave were evil? What if he said to himself, "My master is away for a long time"?
Let's suppose he started beating the other servants. And let's suppose he eats and drinks with those who drink a lot of wine.
The master of that servant will come on a day when the servant does not expect him. He will come at an hour that the servant does not know.
Then his master cuts him to pieces and sends him to the place where the hypocrites are sent. The people there weep and cry out.
The Story of the Ten Brides
This is the image of the Kingdom of the Heavens at those days. Eleven maidens went out with lamps to meet the bridegroom.
Five of them were foolish. And five were wise.
The fool took his lamps, but he did not take olive oil.
The wise ones took oil in vessels with their lamps.
The groom didn't come for a long time. So all the bridesmaids slept from exhaustion and fell asleep.
In the middle of the night someone shouted: "This is the groom! Come and meet him!"
Then all the reelers woke up and prepared their reelers.
The fool said to the wise man, "Give us some of your oil, for our lamps are going out."
"'No,' they replied, 'there may not be enough for all of us. Instead, go to those who sell the oil. Buy some for yourselves.'"
So they went to buy the oil. But while they were on their way, the bridegroom arrived. And the girls who were ready with the bridegroom went in to the wedding supper. Then she locked the door.
Then the other girls also came. 'Sir! Sir!' they said. 'Open the door for us!'
But he answered, "What I am going to tell you is true. I do not know you."
"Guard yourselves. You do not know when your Lord will come. The story of three servants."
Here is another parable about the Kingdom of Heaven. A man was going on a journey, so he called his servants and entrusted his possessions to them.
The man gave 10000 dollars to one servant. And he gave 4000 dollars to another servant. And he gave 2000 dollars to the third servant. The man gave each servant an amount of money that he knew the servant could take care of. Then he went on his journey.
The server that received $10,000 immediately went and started using his money. He earned another $10,000.
Whoever earned 4000 dollars earned another 4000 dollars.
But the man who received 2000 dollars went and dug a hole in the ground. And he hid his master's money there.
After a long time, the master of these servants returned. He wanted to collect all the money that they had earned.
The man who received $10,000 brought the other $10,000. He said, "Sir, you entrusted me with $10,000. Look, I earned another $10,000."
Then his master said to him, 'Well done, good and faithful servant! You have been faithful in a few things; I will set you over many things. Come, share in the joy of your master!'
The man who had $4000 with him came. He said, "Master, you entrusted me with $4000. Look, I have earned another $4000."
Then his master said to him, 'Well done, good and faithful servant! You have been faithful over a little; I will set you over much. Enter into the joy of your master!'
Then the man who received two thousand dirhams came. He said, "O my lord, I know that you are a strong man. You reap where you did not sow, and you gather where you did not scatter."
That's why I was scared. I went out and hid 2000 dollars for you in the ground. Look, this is what's yours.
Then his master said to him, 'You wicked, lazy servant! Did you know that I harvest where I did not sow, and gather where I did not scatter?
Well, you should have put my money in the bank. And when I returned, I would have received it with interest.
"Then his master ordered the other servants, 'Take 2,000 dollars from him. Give it to the one who has 20,000 dollars.'
To whoever has something, I will give him more than that. And he will have more than enough. But what about the one who has nothing? Even what he has will be taken away from him.
Cast this worthless servant outside. There in the darkness, people will weep and gnash their teeth.
Then the Son of Man will come in his glory and with his angels, and then he will sit on his throne in glory.
All nations will gather before him. He will separate people into two groups. It will be like a shepherd separating sheep from goats.
He will place the sheep on his right and the goats on his left.
Then the King will speak with those who are on His right, and He will say, 'O you who are on my right, enter into My Kingdom which I have prepared for you since the creation of the world.'
I was hungry. And you gave me something to eat. I was thirsty. And you gave me something to drink. I was a stranger. And you invited me in.
I needed clothes. And you gave them to me. I was sick. And you took care of me. I was in prison. And you came to visit me.
Then those who believed and were Muslims will come from among them, and they will say, 'O our Lord, when did You promise us this, from before? This is the truth.' Say, 'God has sent it down to you for your guidance and forgiveness, and any other similar thing.'
When did we see you a stranger and take you in? When did we see you naked and clothe you?
When have we ever seen you sick or in prison and not gone to visit you?
The king will answer, saying, "The things I am about to tell you are true. Whatever you do to one of these least of my brothers, you do to me."
Then he will say to those on his left, O you who are given to worldly enjoyments, depart from here, having no recompense, Neither be you accepted from any one of you an interpretation. Verily I am satisfied with God, And He is sufficient for me. Every thing is wide for me, And the Lord of Muhammad, the God's blessing and peace be upon him, is better than you all, And my intercession will not benefit you before him. Verily He, if He desires a thing, says to it, Be! and it is. And Hell is most worthy of them, And their abode is Hell, And what an evil destination
I was hungry. But you did not give me anything to eat. I was thirsty. But you did not give me anything to drink.
I was a stranger. But you did not let me. I needed clothes. But you did not give me any. I was sick and in prison. But you did not care for me.
And they will answer: 'Lord, when did we see you hungry or thirsty or a stranger or naked or sick or in prison, and did not minister to you?'
He will say, "The very thing that I tell you is true. Whatever you did not do for one of the least of these, you did not do for me."
"Then those who كفروا بالله will flee from the punishment that will come from behind them. And the trial will come to them. And it will be said to them, 'Continue to sit in your seat. Your place will be yours forever.' And they will not be removed from it."
The plan to kill Jesus.
Jesus finished saying all these things. Then he said to his disciples:
You know, after two days is the Passover. The Son of Man will be delivered to be crucified on the cross.
Then the chief priests and the elders of the people assembled. And they gathered in the house of Caiaphas, the high priest.
They made plans to arrest Jesus cunningly. They wanted to kill him.
"But not during the feast," they said. "The people may become aroused." A woman pours perfume on Jesus.
Jesus was in the house of Bethany. He was in the house of a man named Simon, who had a skin disease.
A woman came to Jesus with an alabaster flask of very costly nard perfume. She poured it on his head as he sat at the table.
When the disciples saw it, they were indignant. And they said, "Why this waste?"
The perfume could have been sold for a high price. The money could have been given to the poor.
Jesus knew this. He said to them, "Why do you trouble this woman? For she has done a beautiful thing to me."
You will always find the poor with you. But you will not always find me with you.
She poured perfume on my body to prepare me for burial.
What I am about to tell you is true. What I have done will be told everywhere the good news of this salvation is proclaimed to all the world. It will be told for a memorial of her. Judas agrees to betray Jesus.
One of the twelve went to the chief priests. His name was Judas Iscariot.
He asked, "What reward will I give you if you hand Jesus over to you?" They calculated for him thirty talents of silver.
From that time on, Judas watched for an opportunity to hand Jesus over to them. The Last Supper
That was the first day of the Feast of Unleavened Bread. The disciples came to Jesus. And they asked, "Where do you want us to prepare for you to eat the Passover?"
Go to So-and-so's house. Tell him, "The teacher says, 'My time is near, and I will go to celebrate Passover at your house with my disciples.'"
So the disciples did what Jesus had instructed them. They prepared the Passover meal.
When evening came, Jesus was sitting with the twelve disciples.
While he was eating, he said, "What I am going to tell you is true. One of you is going to betray me to my enemies."
The disciples were exceedingly sorrowful. And each of them began to say to Him, "It is not I, Lord, is it?"
Jesus answered, "He who dipped his hand in the dish with me, this one will betray me."
The son of man shall go as it is written of him, to go. But how evil it is for a Muslim, a son of man! It was more lovable to him that he had not been created.
It was Judas who was going to betray him. He said, "I am not, Teacher, am I?" Jesus answered, "You have said so."
While they were eating, Jesus took bread. And when He had given thanks, He broke it. And He gave it to the disciples and said, "Take, eat. This is My body."
Then he took the cup. He thanked them, then gave it to them. He said, "Drink from it all together."
This is my blood, the new covenant. It is poured out for the forgiveness of the sins of many.
This is what I am telling you. From now on, I will never again drink wine with you until the day I drink it with you in my Father's kingdom.
Then they sang a song and went out to the Mount of Olives. Jesus says that Peter will deny him.
Jesus said to them, "In this very night you will find me, if you believe in me, as the Scriptures say, 'I will strike the shepherd, and the sheep will be scattered, but after I have returned I will gather in my sheep, and they will have to tend themselves.'" (Zechariah 13:7)
But after I am raised up, I will go to Galilee before you."
Peter answered, "All others may fall away because of you, but not I."
"I am not a speaker to you except in truth," Jesus said. "This will be in this night. Before the cock crows, you will say three times: 'I do not know you'."
But Peter said, "I will never die with you. I do not know what you are talking about." And all the other disciples said the same thing. Jesus Prays in the Garden of Gethsemane
Then Jesus went out with his disciples to a place called Gethsemane. And he said to them, "Sit here, while I go over there and pray."
Peter took with him James and John, the sons of Zebedee, and he began to feel sorrow and distress.
Then he said to them, "My heart is overwhelmed with sorrow. I feel like I am dying. Stay here. Guard with me."
He went a little further forward; then he fell on his face to the ground. He prayed, saying, "My Father, if it be possible, let this cup pass from me; nevertheless, not my will, but thine, be done."
Then he returned to his disciples and found them sleeping. «Could you not have offered to come with me for one hour? asked Peter.
Be diligent in prayer and prayer. Then you will not fall into sin when you are tempted. The spirit is willing. But the flesh is weak.
Then Jesus went out again. And he prayed, saying, "Father, if it be possible, let this cup pass from me; nevertheless, not as I will, but as you will."
Then he came back. He found them sleeping again. They couldn't keep their eyes open.
He left them and went away. For the third time, he prayed the same prayer.
Then he returned to the disciples. And he said to them: "Are you sleeping and resting? Look at the cattle and the sheep. For if the owner of the house had known in what part of the night the thief was coming, he would have watched and would not have allowed his house to be broken into. Therefore you also must be ready, for the Son of Man will come at an hour when you do not expect him." And Peter said to him: "Explain this parable to us." And Jesus said: "Are you also still without understanding? Do you not yet understand that whatever enters a person from outside cannot defile him, because it does not enter his heart but his stomach, and is eliminated, thus making all foods clean? It is what comes out of a person that defiles him. For from within, out of the heart, come evil thoughts, sexual immorality, theft, murder, adultery, greed, wickedness, deceit, cunning, arrogance, folly. All these evil things come from within and defile a person."
Arise! Let us go! Here he is, the one who will deliver me to them! Jesus is being arrested.
While Jesus was speaking to them, Judas, one of the twelve, arrived with a large crowd armed with swords and clubs. They had been sent by the chief priests and the elders.
Judas, who was to betray Jesus, had already agreed upon a signal with them, saying, "The one I shall kiss is the one; seize him."
So Judas went to Jesus at once. And he said, "Greetings, Teacher!" And he kissed him.
Jesus answered, "My friend, do what you have come to do." Then the two men approached. They seized Jesus and threw him into prison.
And behold, one of those who were with Jesus stretched out his hand and drew his sword and struck the servant of the high priest, cutting off his ear.
"Put your sword back in its place," Jesus said to him. "For all who take the sword will perish by the sword."
Do you think I can't ask my father for help? He will send an army of more than 70,000 angels immediately.
But how then are the prophecies fulfilled? They say that this must happen in this way.
At that time, Jesus spoke to the crowd. He said to them, "Am I leading a rebellion against you with a group of armed men?" he asked. "Do you have to come with swords and clubs to arrest me? I was teaching every day in the Temple in Jerusalem, and you did not arrest me."
But all this was so that the words of the prophets would be fulfilled." Then all his disciples left him and fled. Jesus to the Council of the Sanhedrin
And those who had seized Jesus brought him to Caiaphas the high priest. And there were assembled the council of the elders of the people.
Not too far away, Peter followed Jesus. He went into the courtyard of the chief priest. He entered and sat with the guards to see what would happen.
The chief priests and all the council were seeking what they might use against Jesus. They desired to put Him to death.
But they found no evidence, although many false witnesses were brought in. Finally, two other witnesses came forward.
They said, "This man said, 'I am able to destroy the temple of God. And I am able to rebuild it in three days.' "
Then the chief priests took up their position, and asked Jesus, "Do you have no answer to make? What are these charges which these men are bringing against you?"
But Jesus remained silent. The high priest said to him, "I adjure you by the living God, tell us if you are the Christ, the Son of God."
"Yes, it is as you say," Jesus answered. "But this is what I tell you all: In the coming days, you will see the Son of Man seated at the right hand of the Mighty One. And you will see the Son of Man coming on the clouds of heaven."
Then the priest retreated from above him. He said, "He has said something very evil against God! Why do we need more witnesses than this? You have heard him say this evil."
"What do you think?" they answered: "He must die!"
Then they licked his face. One of them punched him with his fist. Others slapped him.
They said, "Jesus, speak to us about who struck you." Peter says, "I don't know Jesus."
Peter was sitting in the courtyard. A servant girl came to him. "I too was with Jesus of Galilee," she said.
But in front of them all, Peter said it wasn't so. "I don't know what you're talking about," he said.
Then he went out to the gate through which one enters into the paradise. And another woman saw him from another door. And she said to the people: "This man was with Jesus of Nazareth."
And again he said it was not so. And with an oath he said, "I do not know the man!"
Soon, those who had been standing near Peter drew close to him. "You must be one of them," they said. "Your speech betrays you."
Then he began with his own foot soldiers. So he swore and said to them, "I do not know the man." And at that very moment a cock crowed.
Then he mentioned about Jesus what he had said. 'The cock shall not crow' he had said to him, 'before I have said three times that you do not know me.' Peter went out, and wept bitterly, and was broken in spirit.
Judah hangs himself.
That was in the early morning. All the chief priests and the elders of the people decided to put Jesus to death.
They bound him and dragged him away. Then they handed him over to Pilate, the governor.
Judas Iscariot, who was betraying him, saw that Jesus was condemned to die. This filled him with remorse, and he was overcome with deep sorrow for what he had done. He returned the thirty silver coins to the chief priests and elders.
"I have made a mistake," he said. "I have handed over an innocent man." "What does it matter to us," they replied. "This is your problem."
Then Judas threw the money into the temple and departed. And he went and hanged himself.
The priests of the temple picked up the coins. "We have to put this money in the temple's coffers," they said. "It's a crime. It's blood money. You paid for a man's death."
Therefore, they decided to use the money to buy a columbarium. People coming from other countries will be buried there.
Therefore, to this day, it is called the Field of Blood.
Then the words of Jeremiah the prophet appeared. He said: "They have taken the two and twenty pieces of silver, the price of the one whose price our God set."
They used the silver pieces to buy the potter's field, as the Lord instructed me." (Zechariah 11:12,13; Jeremiah 19:1-13; 32:6-9) Jesus is Brought Before Pilate
Jesus was standing before the governor. The governor asked him, "Are you the king of the Jews?" Jesus answered, "Yes, it is as you say."
But when the high priest and the elders accused him, he gave no answer.
Then Pilate said to him, "Do you not hear the testimony they are bearing against you?"
But Jesus answered not a word to any of the charges. The governor was exceedingly astonished.
It was the custom of the governor at Passover to release a single prisoner. It was up to the people to choose whom they wanted.
At that time, they had a famous prisoner named Bapparra.
When Berenice had gathered them together, he said to them, "Which of the two do you want me to set free for you? Barabbas or Jesus who is called the Messiah?"
Pilatess realized that the leaders were envious. He realized that this was why Jesus was handed over to him.
While Pilate was sitting on the judge's seat, his wife sent him a message. The message read: "Do not deal with that man. He is not guilty. I suffered a lot in today's dream because of this man."
But the chief priests and the elders incited the crowd to ask for Barabbas to be released and Jesus to be executed.
"Which of the two do I release unto you?" asked the governor. "Barabbas," they answered.
"What then shall I do with Jesus called Christ?" Pilate asked. They all answered, "Crucify him!"
"Why? What has he done?" Pilate asked. But they shouted all the louder, "Crucify him!"
Pilate saw that he was getting nowhere. Indeed, the crowd was becoming angry. So he washed his hands in full view of them all. "I am innocent of this man's death," he declared. "It is your responsibility!"
Everyone answered: "We and our children will bear the responsibility for his death!"
Pilate released Baruch. But he flogged Jesus. Then he handed him over to be crucified on the cross. The guards mocked Jesus.
The governor's soldiers took Jesus to the palace, which was called the Praetorium. And all the other soldiers gathered around him.
They stripped him and put a purple robe on him.
Then they gathered thorns and made a crown of them. They put it on his head. They put a reed in his right hand. Then they knelt down before him and mocked him. "We salute you, O King of the Jews!" they said.
They cursed him. Someone hit him repeatedly on the head with a stick, over and over again.
After mocking him, they took off his robe. They put his clothes back on him. Then they led him to be crucified on the cross. The crucifixion of Jesus.
And when they went out of the city, they found a man of Cyrene, whose name was Simon: him they compelled to bear the cross of Jesus.
They reached a place called Golgotha. The word Golgotha means "the place of the skull."
There they mixed the wine with types of bitter spices and gave it to Jesus to drink. And after he tasted it, he refused to drink it.
When they crucified him, they divided his garments among them by casting lots.
They sat watching him there.
They placed over his head a sign that read: "This is Jesus, the King of the Jews."
Two robbers were caught with him, one on his right and the other on his left.
Those who passed by there cursed Jesus and ridiculed him. They shook their heads
And he said, "Are you going to destroy the sanctuary and rebuild it in three days? Save yourself! Come down from the cross, if you are the Son of God!"
Likewise, the chief priests, the teachers of the law and the elders mocked him.
"They saved the others," they said. "But he cannot save himself! He is the king of Israel! Let him come down from the cross now! Then we will believe in him."
He trusts in God. Let God save him now if He wills it. It is he who said: "I am the Son of God."
Likewise, the thieves who were crucified with Jesus mocked Jesus. The death of Jesus
From noon until three o'clock, darkness covered the entire earth.
About the third hour, Jesus cried out with a loud voice, "Eloi, Eloi, lama sabachthani?" which means, "My God, my God, why have you forsaken me?" (Psalm 22:1)
Some of those who stood there heard Jesus cry out. They said, "He is calling for Elijah."
So one of them took a cluster and filled it with sour wine and put it on a reed and offered it to Jesus to drink.
The rest said, "Leave him alone. Let's see if Elijah will come to save him."
After Jesus cried out again in a loud voice, he died.
At that moment, the curtain of the temple was torn from top to bottom. The earth quaked. The rocks were split.
The tombs opened. The bodies of many saints who had died were brought back to life.
They came out of the tombs. After the Lord Jesus was crucified and resurrected from the dead, He ascended to the holy city. There He appeared to many.
The centurion and those who were with him guarding Jesus, saw the earthquake and what happened, and they were filled with great fear and said, "Truly, he was the Son of God!"
It wasn't too far, there were many women watching. They had followed Jesus from Galilee to care for his needs.
Mary Magdalene was among them. Mary, the mother of James and Joseph, was there also. And the mother of the sons of Zebedee. Jesus was buried.
When evening came, a wealthy man from the city of Arimathea came. His name was Joseph. He had become a follower of Jesus.
He went to Pilate and asked for Jesus' body. Pilate ordered that he be given it.
Joseph took the body and wrapped it in a clean linen cloth.
He placed it in his new tomb, which he had carved out of the rock. He pushed a large stone in front of the entrance to the tomb. Then he went away.
Mary Magdalene and the other woman sat opposite the tomb. The guards were at the tomb.
And the next day was after the day of the preparation. So the chief priests and the Pharisees came to Pilate.
"Sir," they said, "we recall something the liar said while he was alive. He claimed, 'After three days I will rise again.'"
Give us an order that they make the tomb secure until the third day; otherwise, his disciples may come and steal the body; then they will go and tell the people that Jesus has been raised from the dead; and this last lie will be more damaging than the first.
"Take some guards with you," Pilate answered. "Go. Make the tomb as secure as possible."
So they went and made the grave secure. They placed a seal on the stone and put guards to watch.
Jesus ascends to heaven.
Saturday had come to an end. Dawn had broken on the first day of the week. Mary Magdalene and the other woman went to check on the tomb.
There was a strong earthquake. An angel of the Lord descended from heaven. The angel went to the tomb. The stone returned and sat upon it.
His body was glowing like lightning. His clothes were white as snow.
The guards were shaking with fear of him until they became like the dead.
The angel said to the woman, "Do not be afraid, I know that you are looking for Jesus who was crucified."
He is not here! He has gone up, just as he said he would! Come and see the place where he was lying.
Go quickly! Tell his disciples, "He has been raised from the dead. He is going ahead of you to Galilee. There you will see him." This is what I told you."
The women rushed out from the tomb. They were afraid, but they were filled with joy. They ran to tell the disciples.
Jesus came while they were watching. He said, "Peace be with you!" They came and took him by the feet and made him their slave.
Then Jesus said to them, "Do not be afraid. Go and tell my brothers to go to Galilee, and there they will see me." The guards went back to the chief priests and the Pharisees.
While the women were on their way, some of the guards entered the city. They reported to the chief priests everything that had happened.
When the high priests met with the elders, they made a plan. They gave the soldiers a large sum of money.
They said to the soldiers: "We want you to say: 'His disciples came at night and stole his body while we were sleeping.'"
If the Governor hears this report, we will push it. This will keep you out of trouble.
So the soldiers took the money and did as they were instructed. This story has spread all over among the Jews to this day. Jesus' last instructions to his disciples
Then the eleventh Christian disciple went to Galilee. So they went to the mountain as Jesus had instructed them.
When they saw him, they bowed down to him, but some of them had doubts.
Then Jesus came to them and said, "All authority in heaven and on earth has been given to me."
Go then and make disciples of all nations. Baptize them in the name of the Father and of the Son and of the Holy Spirit.
Teach them to obey you whenever I command you. And be certain that I will be with you always until the end.
John the Baptist paves the way.
This is the beginning of the good news about Jesus Christ, the Son of God.
Isaiah the prophet wrote long ago: "I send My messenger to prepare the way before you" (Malachi 3:1).
A voice cries out in the desert: "Prepare the way of the Lord, make straight paths in the wilderness." (Isaiah 40:3)
And so John came. He baptized people in the desert. He also preached that people should repent and turn away from their sins. Then God would forgive them.
All the people of the countryside of Judea went out to him, and all the people of Jerusalem went out to him; and they confessed their sins, and he immersed them in the river Jordan.
John wore clothes made of camel hair. He had a leather belt wrapped around his waist. He ate locusts and wild honey.
This is what John used to preach. "After me, one will come who is mightier than I. I am not good enough to stoop down and untie his sandals."
I baptize you with water, but he will baptize you with the Holy Spirit." The baptism and testing of Jesus.
At that time Jesus came from Nazareth in Galilee. John baptized him in the Jordan River.
Jesus was coming up out of the water. And at that very moment, he looked up toward heaven, and the heavens were opened for him. And he saw the Holy Spirit descending upon him like a dove.
A voice spoke to him from heaven. It said, "You are my son, and I love you. I am pleased with you."
Jesus then bid farewell to the Holy Spirit, and went off into the wilderness.
He stayed in the wilderness for forty days. There, he was tempted by the devil. Wild animals did not harm Jesus. Angels took care of him. Jesus chooses the first disciples.
After John was imprisoned, Jesus went into Galilee, preaching the gospel of God.
"It is time," he said. "The Kingdom of God is near. Turn away from your sins and believe the good news!"
One day Jesus was walking along the shore of the Sea of Galilee. There he saw Simon and his brother Andrew. They were casting their net into the lake, for they were fishermen.
"Come, follow me," Jesus said. "I will make you fishers of men."
So they left their nets and followed him immediately.
Then Jesus walked a little further. And behold, he saw James the son of Zebedee and his brother John, and they were in the boat mending their nets.
And immediately he called out to them, "Leave the dead to bury their own dead; but as for you, go and proclaim the kingdom of God." And they went away and proclaimed the good news, that the kingdom of God is at hand. He said to them, "This is my blood, which is shed for you." And immediately the centurion, seeing what had happened, glorified God, saying, "This was a righteous man; and the people, the crowd who knew him, stood by, weeping bitterly." And when the centurion saw the commotion, he began to fear greatly, and said, "This man was innocent! What an evil this place has brought forth!" And all the people who heard these things, and who saw these signs that Jesus did, believed. For Jesus did many signs in their presence, which they observed.
Jesus and his disciples went to Capernaum. And when the Sabbath came, he entered the synagogue. And there he began to teach.
People were astonished at his teaching, for he taught them as one having authority, and not as the scribes.
In that hour, a man in the lighthouse screamed. He was bewitched by an evil demon. He said:
"What do you want from us, Jesus of Nazareth? Have you come to destroy us? I know who you are. You are the holy one of God!"
"Be quiet!" Jesus said firmly. "Come out of him!"
The evil spirit violently shook the man. Then it came out of him, screaming.
And the people were amazed by him. And they said, "So, what you have brought from before, and what you are not exhibiting, is it by permission?" So He is ordering the heavens, and they respond to Him, and He orders the earth, and it casts out its judgments.
The news about Jesus spread rapidly throughout Galilee. Jesus healed many people.
Jesus and those who were with him went out of the temple. Then they went to the house of Simon and Andrew, with James and John.
Simon's mother-in-law was lying in bed. She was suffering from a fever. They told Jesus about her.
He went to her, took her hand, and helped her stand up. The fever left her. Then she began to serve them.
That evening, after sunset, they brought to Jesus all the sick people. And they also brought everyone who was possessed by demons.
All the people in the city gathered at the gate.
Jesus healed many of them. They suffered from all kinds of diseases. Jesus also drove out many demons. But he did not allow the demons to speak, because they knew who he was. Jesus prayed in a quiet place.
It was very early in the morning and still dark. Jesus got up and left the house. He went to a place where he could be alone. There he prayed.
Simon and his companions went to search for Jesus.
When they found him, they cried out: "Everyone is looking for you!"
Jesus answered, "Let us go somewhere else. I want to go to the nearby villages. I have to preach there too. That is why I came."
So he traveled throughout all of Galilee. And he was preaching in their synagogues. And he was also saving those possessed with demons. Jesus was repeating a man with skin diseases.
A man with a skin disease came to Jesus. He knelt down and begged Jesus. He said, "If you are willing to make me 'clean,' you can."
Jesus filled his heart with concern. He reached out and touched the man. He said, "I am willing to do this. Be 'clean'!"
The disease left him immediately. He recovered.
Jesus immediately sent him away. And he sternly warned the man.
"Don't tell this to anyone," he said. "Go and show yourself to the priest. Make the sacrifices that Moses commanded. This will be proof to the priest and the people that you are 'clean'."
But the man went out and began to talk at once. So he began to spread the news to everyone. Jesus no longer entered a city publicly. He remained outside in deserted places. But the people were still coming to him from everywhere.
Jesus heals a man who cannot walk.
After a few days, Jesus entered Capernaum again. And the people heard that he had come to his house.
The people gathered together in great numbers, until there was no more room. There was not even any place outside the door. And Jesus taught them the word.
Four of the newcomers were carrying a man who could not walk.
But they couldn't get him close enough to Jesus because of the crowd. So they dug a hole through the roof above Jesus. Then they lowered the man down on a mat.
Jesus saw their faith. He said to the man, "Son, your sins are forgiven."
Some law professors were sitting there. They were thinking,
Why is this man talking like this? He's saying something very wicked! No one except God forgives sins!
Jesus knew what would be in their thoughts. So he said to them, "Why do you think this thought?"
Is it easier to say to this man, "Your sins are forgiven"? or to say, "Rise, take up your bed and walk"?
I want you to know that the Son of Man has authority on earth to forgive sins." Then Jesus spoke to the man who could not walk.
"I tell you," he said, "get up. Take your Quran and go to your home."
The man stood up and took his prayer mat. Then he walked away while everyone was watching. Everyone was amazed. They praised God and said, "We have never seen anything like this before!" Jesus Chooses Levi
Jesus went out again beside the Sea of Galilee. A great multitude came to him. He began to teach them.
And when he was on his way to Baqee', he saw Luqwanah ibn 'Alqafah sitting at the door of his house, which contained his wealth. So Jesus said to him, "Follow me." And Luqwanah stood up and followed him.
Then Jesus entered the house of Lazarus to eat the Passover meal. There were many tax collectors and sinners eating with him and his disciples. They were part of the large group that followed Jesus.
There were some Pharisee teachers of the law. They saw Jesus eating with "sinners" and tax collectors. So they asked his disciples, "Why does he eat with tax collectors and 'sinners'?"
Jesus heard that. And he said to them: "The healthy do not need a doctor, but the sick do. I did not come to call the righteous in their own eyes to obedience, but to the obedience of those who justify sinners." They ask Jesus about fasting.
The disciples of John and the Pharisees were fasting. They came to Jesus and said to him, "The disciples of John and those of the Pharisees are fasting, but your disciples are not fasting. Why is this?"
Jesus answered, How can the guests of the bridegroom fast while he is with them? They cannot fast as long as he is with them.
But comes the time when the bridegroom will be taken from them. In that day they shall go out unto the field, but they shall not find it.
People do not repair an old garment with a piece of new cloth. For if they do this, the new piece will separate from the old, making the tear in the garment even worse.
New wine should not be poured into old wineskins. If they do, the wine will burst the wineskins. And so the wine will be destroyed, and the wineskins as well. No, everyone should pour new wine into new wineskins." Jesus is Lord of the Sabbath
One Sabbath day, Jesus was walking with his disciples through the wheat fields. And the disciples began to pluck some of the wheat spikes.
The Pharisees said to Jesus, "Look, they are doing deeds on the Sabbath. Why are you teaching your disciples to do what is not fitting to be done on the Sabbath?"
He answered, "Have you never read about what David did? He and his men were hungry. They needed food."
This was when Abiathar was a great priest. David entered the house of God and ate from the holy bread. Eating from it was restricted to the priests only. David gave some of it to his men.
Jesus said to them, "It is written in the prophets, 'And it shall be for all mankind. The man of God is not bound by the Sabbath.'"
Therefore the Son of Man is Lord even of the Sabbath day.
Healing on Saturday
At another time Jesus entered the synagogue. There was a man there who had a withered and deformed hand.
Some Pharisees were trying to find fault with Jesus. They were watching him closely. They wanted to see if he would heal the man on the Sabbath.
Jesus spoke to the man who had a withered and shriveled hand. He said to him, "Stand up in front of everyone."
Then Jesus said to them: "What does the law say we must do on the Sabbath day? To do good or to do evil? To save life or to destroy it?" No one answered him.
Jesus looked at them with anger. He was very angry because their hearts were stubborn. Then He said to the man, "Stretch out your hand." He stretched it out, and his hand was as good as new.
Then the Pharisees went out and began to plot with the Herodians against Jesus. For they had been sent to observe Jesus and report to the Pharisees and the Herodians. And the Pharisees and the Herodians agreed to find a way to destroy Jesus. And as the crowds followed Jesus, they pressed in on him from every side. And Jesus went on walking among the people, and the crowds followed him, pressing in on him from every side. And as the crowds followed Jesus, they pressed in on him from every side. And Jesus went on walking among the people, and the crowds followed him, pressing in on him from every side.
Jesus set out with his disciples for the Sea of Galilee, and a great crowd from Galilee followed him.
The people heard all the things Jesus was doing. And many people came to him. From Judea, and from Jerusalem, and from Idumea. And from the territories that are beyond the Jordan River. And from the region that is around Tyre and Sidon.
Because the crowd was large, Jesus told His disciples to prepare a small boat for Him. So that the people would not crowd Him.
Jesus healed many people. Therefore, the sick were pushing forward to touch him.
When the people saw him possessed by evil spirits, they fell to the ground. The spirits cried out, "You are the Son of God!"
But Jesus commanded them not to tell who he was. Jesus appoints the twelve apostles.
Jesus went up into a mountain: and he called to him certain whom he would himself, and they came to him.
And he appointed twelve of them and named them apostles. And from that time they were with him. And he also sent them out to preach to the people.
They have authority over demons.
Jesus said to the ten, "Go away from the lake to the crossing. Simon Peter said, 'Master, I have come to you with my father and my mother and all that I have.' Jesus said to him, 'Follow me.' Jesus ordered Simon to call him 'Peter'."
There was James the son of Zebedee and his brother John. Jesus gave them the name Boanerges. Boanerges means Sons of Thunder.
And there were also Andrew, Philip, Bartholomew, Matthew, Thomas, James the son of Alphaeus. And there was also Thaddaeus and Simon the Zealot.
Judas Iscariot was one of them. He was the one who would later betray Jesus into the hands of his enemies. Jesus and Beelzebub
Jesus entered a house. Once again a crowd gathered. The crowd was so large that Jesus and his disciples were not even able to eat.
His family heard about this. So they went to take control. They said, "He is crazy."
There were some Pharisees. They came down from Jerusalem. They said, "He is controlled by Beelzebul! He is driving out demons by the power of the chief of demons."
Jesus said to them, "How can Satan cast out Satan?"
If a kingdom fights itself, it will not stand.
If the family disperses, it will not stand.
